WEBVTT

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.466
Can a robot learn to recover from its own mistakes?

00:00:03.726 --> 00:00:07.605
FailPoint-X studies offline post-training from human takeovers.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:13.208
The black book belongs in the front compartment of the caddy. The
robot has already missed.

00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:16.466
Watch two policies restart from exactly that moment.

00:00:16.726 --> 00:00:23.061
In this replay, the base policy times out. The policy trained on
human recoveries puts the book away.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.026
An overall success rate hides whether the robot avoided a mistake
or recovered after making it.

00:00:29.286 --> 00:00:33.984
And extra training alone might help. How much do the human
corrections contribute?

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.431
We record failures from one checkpoint. An annotator marks the
interval where each failure begins.

00:00:40.691 --> 00:00:45.715
A human takes over from sampled states in training intervals and
finishes the original task.

00:00:45.975 --> 00:00:50.505
We match the training budget, then restart every method at the same
held-out midpoints.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:57.405
Across ten task families and five levels, we collect 2,400 accepted
human recoveries.

00:00:57.665 --> 00:01:03.663
Eighty are reserved for development. Nine methods face the same 384
held-out failure states.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:11.597
Demo-continue recovers in 22.5 percent of trials. Takeover-BC
recovers in 51.9 percent.

00:01:11.857 --> 00:01:17.231
At equal training budgets, that is a 29.4 percentage point gain on
levels one through three.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:24.934
From ordinary starts, Base-restart succeeds in 43.9 percent of
trials.

00:01:25.194 --> 00:01:31.576
Takeover-BC succeeds in 14 percent of trials. Recovery and
retention tell different stories.
